WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.468
A binary classifier begins with a score: a real number expressing which side of its decision

00:00:06.468 --> 00:00:12.737
boundary an input lies on. Sigmoid converts that score into a class-1 probability. The score and

00:00:12.737 --> 00:00:17.830
probability are different quantities, so they have different allowed ranges and different meanings.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:23.228
A score can be negative or greater than one. It is not yet a probability, so

00:00:23.228 --> 00:00:26.593
taking the logarithm of the score would be the wrong operation.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:33.340
The exponential is positive, so the denominator exceeds one. This keeps the output strictly between zero

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.680
and one for any finite score.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:41.861
Keep the score fixed and change the observed class. Which quantities will change? Pause here and

00:00:41.861 --> 00:00:43.630
make a prediction before continuing.

00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:54.755
The model has two complementary probabilities. The loss uses the one assigned to the class that

00:00:54.755 --> 00:01:04.777
actually occurred. For our starting inputs, Class 1 probability: 0.75026. True-class probability: 0.75026. Negative log loss:

00:01:04.777 --> 00:01:05.403
0.28734.

00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:13.392
Taking the negative logarithm produces a nonnegative penalty. A small true-class probability creates a large penalty;

00:01:13.392 --> 00:01:15.723
increasing it reduces the loss.

00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:23.049
Trace z → sigmoid(z) → probability of the observed class → negative logarithm. Do not take

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:26.883
the logarithm of the score or forget the target.

